  <!-- SECTION 6: RESIDENTIAL SOLAR PANELS -->
  <div class="blog-section" id="solar-roi">
    <h2>6. How to Calculate ROI on Solar Panels in 2026</h2>
    <p>
      With rising retail electricity rates across California, Texas, New York, and Michigan, solar power remains one of the most popular residential capital improvements. In 2026, calculating solar ROI revolves around the <strong>30% Federal Residential Clean Energy Credit (IRC Section 25D)</strong>, local utility net metering tariffs (such as California NEM 3.0), and annual utility rate escalation.
    </p>

    <div class="blog-code">
      <code><span class="comment">// Solar System ROI &amp; Payback Period</span></code>
      <code>Net Turnkey Cost = Gross System Cost - 30% Federal ITC - State/Utility Rebates</code>
      <code>Simple Payback (Years) = <span class="hl">Net Turnkey Cost</span> / <span class="res">Year 1 Electric Utility Bill Savings</span></code>
      <code>25-Year Solar ROI (%) = [ (<span class="hl">Cumulative 25-Year Savings - Net Cost</span>) / <span class="res">Net Cost</span> ] × 100</code>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Worked Example: 9 kW Residential System in Phoenix or Dallas</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gross Installed Cost:</strong> $27,000 ($3.00/Watt)</li>
      <li><strong>30% Federal Tax Credit:</strong> -$8,100 (claimed on IRS Form 5695)</li>
      <li><strong>Net Out-of-Pocket Cost:</strong> $18,900</li>
      <li><strong>Year 1 Utility Bill Offset:</strong> $2,400 per year ($200/mo)</li>
      <li><strong>Estimated Utility Rate Inflation:</strong> 3.5% annually</li>
      <li><strong>Simple Payback Period:</strong> $18,900 / $2,400 = <strong>7.87 Years</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Estimated 25-Year Cumulative Savings:</strong> ~$93,500</li>
      <li><strong>25-Year Net ROI:</strong> ($93,500 - $18,900) / $18,900 × 100 = <strong>394.7% ROI</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      After year 8, the system is fully amortized, providing 17+ years of virtually free, zero-emission electricity while increasing the appraised resale value of the home.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 7: EXCEL ROI GUIDE -->
  <div class="blog-section" id="excel-roi">
    <h2>7. How to Calculate ROI in Excel &amp; Google Sheets (With Formulas)</h2>
    <p>
      Spreadsheets are the industry standard for modeling returns. Here are the exact formulas you need to copy into your Excel or Google Sheets workbooks:
    </p>

    <div class="blog-code">
      <code><span class="comment">// 1. Basic Simple ROI</span></code>
      <code>= (<span class="hl">B2</span> - <span class="res">A2</span>) / <span class="res">A2</span></code>
      <code><span class="comment">Where A2 = Initial Cost, B2 = Final Value or Revenue</span></code>
      <code><br><span class="comment">// 2. Annualized ROI (CAGR)</span></code>
      <code>= ((<span class="hl">B2</span> / <span class="res">A2</span>) ^ (1 / <span class="hl">C2</span>)) - 1</code>
      <code><span class="comment">Where C2 = Number of years held</span></code>
      <code><br><span class="comment">// 3. Built-in Excel RRI Function (Equivalent to CAGR)</span></code>
      <code>= RRI(<span class="hl">C2</span>, <span class="res">A2</span>, <span class="hl">B2</span>)</code>
      <code><br><span class="comment">// 4. Irregular Cash Flow ROI (Internal Rate of Return)</span></code>
      <code>= XIRR(<span class="hl">Values_Range</span>, <span class="res">Dates_Range</span>)</code>
    </div>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Setup in Excel</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Put <code>Initial Investment</code> in cell <strong>A2</strong> (e.g., <code>$25,000</code>).</li>
      <li>Put <code>Final Value</code> in cell <strong>B2</strong> (e.g., <code>$38,000</code>).</li>
      <li>Put <code>Holding Period (Years)</code> in cell <strong>C2</strong> (e.g., <code>3</code>).</li>
      <li>In cell <strong>D2</strong>, enter: <code>=(B2-A2)/A2</code>. Click the <strong>%</strong> button on the Home tab. Result: <strong>52.00%</strong>.</li>
      <li>In cell <strong>E2</strong>, enter: <code>=((B2/A2)^(1/C2))-1</code>. Result: <strong>14.98% per year</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="blog-info">
