  <!-- SECTION 10: 6 COSTLY MISTAKES -->
  <div class="blog-section" id="mistakes">
    <h2>10. Six Costly Mistakes to Avoid When Calculating ROI</h2>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing Revenue With Net Profit:</strong>
        <span>
          A campaign generating $100,000 from $20,000 ad spend is not an $80,000 gain if product sourcing, shipping, labor, and chargebacks totaled $85,000. Always deduct complete cost of goods sold.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring the Time Horizon:</strong>
        <span>
          A 40% gain sounds impressive until you realize it took 12 years to achieve, which yields an underwhelming 2.8% annualized return—easily trailing safe Treasury bills.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Omitting Ongoing Carrying &amp; Maintenance Costs:</strong>
        <span>
          In rental real estate, calculating returns without accounting for 5–8% vacancy allowances, property management, and roof/HVAC replacement reserves leads to catastrophic negative cash flow.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Overlooking State-Specific Taxes:</strong>
        <span>
          A 15% pre-tax capital gain in California or New York will yield significantly less net cash than the same gain in zero-state-income-tax havens like Texas, Florida, or Tennessee.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Failing to Account for Inflation:</strong>
        <span>
          If your investment returned a nominal 6% in a year where consumer inflation (CPI) ran at 3.5%, your real purchasing power growth was only 2.5%.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Double-Counting Tax Deductions and Credits:</strong>
        <span>
          When calculating solar or equipment depreciation (Section 179), make sure you reduce your cost basis accordingly rather than taking duplicate credit in multiple formulas.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 11: FAQS -->
  <div class="blog-section" id="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Calculating ROI</h2>

    <div class="blog-faq-item open">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">What is the formula to calculate ROI?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        The universal ROI formula is: <strong>ROI = ((Final Value of Investment - Initial Cost of Investment) / Initial Cost of Investment) × 100</strong>. For example, if you purchase an asset for $10,000 and sell it for $13,500, your net profit is $3,500. Dividing $3,500 by $10,000 gives 0.35, or a 35% Return on Investment.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">How is ROI calculated on a rental property?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        Real estate investors primarily use Cash-on-Cash Return: <strong>(Annual Pre-Tax Cash Flow / Total Cash Invested) × 100</strong>. Total Cash Invested includes your down payment, closing costs, and upfront renovation expenditures. To calculate total lifecycle ROI, also include tenant mortgage principal paydown, annual property appreciation, and tax shields through depreciation.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">What is the difference between ROI and ROAS?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        ROAS (Return on Ad Spend) measures gross revenue generated directly divided by advertising spend (e.g., $10,000 revenue / $2,000 ad cost = 5.0x ROAS). ROAS completely ignores product manufacturing costs, employee salaries, and overhead. In contrast, ROI subtracts all operating costs to measure net bottom-line profitability.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">How do you calculate ROI in Excel or Google Sheets?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        If your initial investment cost is in cell A2 ($5,000) and the final returned value is in cell B2 ($8,200), type the formula: <code>=(B2-A2)/A2</code> and format the resulting cell as a Percentage. For annualized compound return across C2 years, use: <code>=((B2/A2)^(1/C2))-1</code>.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">What is considered a good ROI in the United States in 2026?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        In 2026, baseline risk-free cash yields in high-yield savings accounts hover around 4.0%–5.0%. As a rule of thumb: S&amp;P 500 index funds average 8%–10% annually, US single-family rental properties aim for 8%–12% cash-on-cash, digital marketing campaigns target 400%+ ROAS (a 5:1 ratio), and Amazon private label products target 100%+ ROI on initial inventory capital.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">What is annualized ROI and when should I use it?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        Annualized ROI calculates the true geometric average yearly rate of return: <code>Annualized ROI = ((1 + Total ROI)^(1 / Number of Years)) - 1</code>. It is essential when comparing investments with different holding durations. A 100% gain over 10 years equals approximately 7.18% per year, whereas a 100% gain over 3 years equals 25.99% per year.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">Can an ROI calculation result in a negative number?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        Yes. If your returned value is less than the total capital invested, your net profit is negative, resulting in a negative ROI. For example, investing $10,000 and recouping only $7,000 yields an ROI of -30.0%, representing a capital loss.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-faq-item">
      <div class="blog-faq-q">How do residential solar tax credits affect solar ROI?</div>
      <div class="blog-faq-a">
        The Federal Residential Clean Energy Credit (Section 25D) provides an uncapped 30% dollar-for-dollar tax credit on the total turnkey installation cost through 2032. On a $25,000 installation, this provides a direct $7,500 tax reduction, reducing net cash outlay to $17,500 and shortening your utility breakeven payback period to 6–8 years.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

